{
  "title": "UPDATED — How does the Node.js event loop work with async/await?",
  "body": "I understand that Node.js is single-threaded but can handle multiple concurrent operations. Can someone explain how async/await interacts with the event loop? I added more context: when we await a promise inside an async function, what happens to the call stack?"
}

{
  "body": "Great question! When you use await inside an async function, the function execution is suspended at that point and control is returned to the event loop. The event loop can then process other tasks while waiting for the promise to resolve. Once the promise resolves, the async function resumes from where it left off. So the thread does NOT pause — it continues processing other operations. This is why Node.js can handle thousands of concurrent connections with a single thread."
}

{
  "body": "I also found this helpful — you can think of await as syntactic sugar over Promises. Under the hood it uses .then() and the microtask queue. The key insight is that async functions always return a Promise, and await unwraps that Promise without blocking the thread."
}

{
  "body": "UPDATED ANSWER: When you use await inside an async function, execution is suspended and control is returned to the event loop. The thread does NOT block — it processes other tasks. Once the awaited Promise resolves, the function resumes. This is powered by Node.js's libuv library which handles I/O operations on separate threads from a thread pool, then reports back to the event loop when done."
}

